White Coat Hypertension
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Consistently elevated office blood pressure | Strictly normal ambulatory/home BP monitoring | Complete absence of hypertensive end-organ damage

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-050
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal distress and extreme anxiety following heavily elevated home blood pressure readings, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude a hypertensive emergency or impending cerebrovascular event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, severely elevated blood pressure accompanied by severe anxiety. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out acute hypertensive encephalopathy, evolving stroke, or acute coronary syndrome, preventing catastrophic target organ damage prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My blood pressure machine says 180 over 100, my heart is racing, and I am absolutely terrified I am about to have a massive stroke.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports highly elevated blood pressure readings that acutely spiked tonight, triggering severe panic. A review of outpatient notes reveals a known history of elevated blood pressure readings in the clinical setting, with normal blood pressure readings consistently taken at home. There is profound transient anxiety and sympathetic arousal during this medical encounter. The patient explicitly denies any severe headache, visual changes, or chest pain. The presentation is completely asymptomatic apart from the acute anxiety surrounding the numerical reading.
Functional Status: Fully independent; highly agitated.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe health anxiety driving compulsive blood pressure checking.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 168/94 mmHg (Initial), 142/84 mmHg (Post-relaxation) | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears flushed and highly anxious, constantly staring at their home blood pressure cuff. A review of a previous separate complaint notes a postural or action tremor with exacerbation by stress/caffeine and an absence of bradykinesia/rigidity (essential tremor), further confirming the patient's baseline high sympathetic tone.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is globally intact; there are absolutely no focal motor or sensory deficits, slurred speech, or facial droop. Fundoscopic examination (if performed) is normal, lacking papilledema. Cardiovascular auscultation reveals a regular tachycardia without murmurs or gallops. There is an absolute lack of clinical signs of left ventricular hypertrophy or acute heart failure (clear lungs, normal JVP).
Environmental Observations: A logbook with multiple blood pressure readings taken every 10 minutes over the last two hours is visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no regular antihypertensives.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: White Coat Hypertension - Status: Acute sympathetic spike secondary to compulsive home monitoring and anxiety.
Differentials:

Hypertensive Emergency - Ruled out definitively due to the complete absence of target organ damage (no visual changes, intact neurology, clear chest, no chest pain).

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out strictly due to the completely normal, symmetric neurological examination.

Pheochromocytoma - Ruled out clinically as the primary cause due to the situational nature of the anxiety and the rapid normalization of the blood pressure once the patient was relaxed and reassured.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s acute, severe distress regarding highly elevated numerical blood pressure readings mandated an immediate after-hours assessment to exclude a life-threatening hypertensive emergency. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the severe risk of impending target organ damage. A meticulous physical and neurological examination confirmed the absolute absence of encephalopathy, stroke, or cardiac ischemia. By definitively establishing that the elevated pressure was purely an asymptomatic sympathetic reaction to anxiety (White Coat/Anxiety-driven Hypertension) rather than a pathological vascular emergency, the clinician safely bypassed a highly inappropriate and potentially dangerous emergency department admission, providing immediate physiological reassurance.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided; patient instructed to cease monitoring for the evening. The expected investigation results include 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring showing a daytime average below 135/85 mmHg, which will assist in confirming the true baseline pressure.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically stop the anxiety cycle driving your blood pressure, permanently place your home blood pressure machine in a high cupboard out of sight for the next week; removing the visual trigger prevents the compulsive checking behavior that is artificially spiking your adrenaline.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: Instruction to have the home blood pressure cuff calibrated at the pharmacy.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days for a formal 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitor referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a sudden, severe, blinding headache, experiences sudden weakness on one side of the body, loses their vision, or develops severe chest pain.

